.po 1i
.TH WHILE 1C UCB
.V 08/15/84 17:10:32
.\"@[$]while.1C	1.5	08/15/84 17:10:32 - Zilog Inc.
.ad
.SH NAME
while \- C Shell flow control statement for loop initiation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwhile (\fIexpression\fB) \fR

	\fIcommand\fR

\fBend\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
While the specified \fIexpression\fR evaluates nonzero,
the commands between the \fBwhile\fR
and the matching \fBend\fR are executed.
\fBBreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR
can be used to terminate or continue the loop prematurely.
The \fBwhile\fR and \fBend\fR must appear on separate lines.
Prompting occurs here the first time through the loop as for the
\fIforeach\fR statement if the input is a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#
\fBwhile ( 1 )\fR	# "1" is always true, therefore
			# this is an endless loop

\fB	echo "This is an endless loop"\fR

			# The string is printed forever
			# or until it is interupted

\fBend\fR		# end of the while loop
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
break(1C), breaksw(1C),
continue(1C), foreach(1C),
if(1C),
onintr(1C), switch(1C),
.br
\fIThe C Shell\fR in the ZEUS Utilities Manual
